(defmodule web/ratelimit
  "Token-bucket rate limiting — a plug that caps how fast a client may hit an endpoint.

  Each key owns a bucket holding up to `:burst` tokens, refilled at `:rate` tokens per
  `:per` ms; a request spends `:cost`. An empty bucket answers `429 Too Many Requests` with
  a `Retry-After` saying when a token frees up. Put it early in a pipeline, so a throttled
  request is rejected before the expensive work:

      (through [(ratelimit/rate-limit {:rate 100 :per 60000 :burst 20})]
        (post \"/login\" auth/login))

  ## Options

    :rate     — tokens replenished per window (default 60)
    :per      — the window in ms (default 60000, i.e. 60/min)
    :burst    — capacity, and so the largest instantaneous burst (default `:rate`)
    :cost     — tokens a request spends: a number, or `(fn (conn) -> number)` (default 1)
    :key-fn   — `(fn (conn) -> key)` choosing the bucket (default `client-key`)
    :skip?    — `(fn (conn) -> bool)`; true exempts the request entirely (default none)
    :store    — where buckets live (default `in-memory-store`)
    :on-limit — `(fn (conn retry-after-seconds) -> conn)` for a custom 429 (default `limited`)

  ## The four seams

  Rate limiting lives in the framework because an app that cannot throttle its own login
  form is incomplete. What keeps that from being a straitjacket is that every part of the
  decision is replaceable.

  **Who is being limited** — `:key-fn`. `client-key` keys by the client address a proxy
  named; `network-key` keys an IPv6 caller by its /64, because an address is not a client
  and a caller can rotate the host half faster than a bucket refills. Your own is a
  function of the conn: an account id, an API token, a tenant, or a constant for one shared
  ceiling.

  **Whether to limit at all** — `:skip?`. Route-level wiring is coarse; this is per request.
  Exempt a health probe, an internal caller, an address allow-list. Never limit the
  platform's health check: throttling it does not protect the machine, it causes the outage
  the check exists to detect.

  **What a request costs** — `:cost` as a function. A request for a hundred rows and one for
  a single row should not spend the same token, and an endpoint whose work scales with a
  parameter can charge for it.

  **Where the buckets live** — `:store`, a map of one function:

      {:take (fn (key cost cfg now) -> :ok | [:deny retry-ms])}

  `cfg` is `{:rate :per :burst}` as `bucket-config` resolves it, and `now` is epoch ms from
  the caller — passed in rather than read inside, so an alternative store is testable at a
  time of the test's choosing. `refill` and `spend` are public so a store can reuse the
  token-bucket arithmetic rather than reimplement it; a Postgres- or Redis-backed store is
  roughly those two functions around a row.

  ## What this does not do, and will not pretend to

  **Buckets are per node.** The default store is one process per machine, so a client
  round-robined across N machines gets N times the configured allowance — measured on a
  two-machine deployment as a burst of 20 admitting 40. This is a deliberate trade: the
  decision sits on the request path, and a shared store spends a network round trip on every
  limited request to avoid over-admitting by the machine count. `web/cache` refuses the same
  trade for the same reason. Divide your ceiling by the machine count, or supply a `:store`
  when you need a ceiling you can prove.

  **Buckets are never evicted.** The table is keyed by whatever `:key-fn` returns, so a key
  taken from user input grows it without bound. `stats` reports `:buckets` for exactly this
  reason — a steadily climbing count is that bug before it is a memory problem.

  **It fails open.** If the limiter does not answer within a second the request is allowed.
  The worst case of admitting a window of traffic is a busy server; the worst case of the
  alternative is an outage caused by the thing meant to prevent one.

  **An address is a weak identity.** `client-key` trusts a proxy header (see
  `web/auth/client-ip`), which is only meaningful because the proxy overwrites it. Read it
  from a request that did not come through one and it is whatever the caller typed. This is
  an outer defence, never the only one.

  ## Seeing it work

  `stats` gives `{:allowed :denied :deny-rate :buckets}`, which `web/dashboard` renders, and
  every rejection emits `[:hatch :ratelimit :denied]` telemetry carrying the key, the cost,
  the method and the path — so a 429 storm is visible to a metrics pipeline rather than only
  in the access log."
  ; halt / send-resp / put-resp-header / header — reject a throttled request
  (:use web/conn)
  ; ensure-supervised — the buckets live in a supervised singleton
  (:use web/registry)
  ; client-ip — the one implementation of "which header names the real client"
  (:alias web/auth)
  ; emit — a rejection is worth seeing; web/metrics and a dashboard read this
  (:alias telemetry)
  ; the counters behind `stats`
  (:alias table))

; ---- the bucket process: key -> {:tokens float :ts ms-epoch} ----

(defn refill (bucket cfg dnow)
  "Refill `bucket` to time `dnow`: add the tokens accrued since its timestamp, capped at the
  bucket's capacity (`:burst`). A fresh key starts full."
  (let (burst (get cfg :burst)
        per-ms (/ (get cfg :rate) (* 1.0 (get cfg :per)))
        tokens (get bucket :tokens (* 1.0 burst))
        ts (get bucket :ts dnow)
        refilled (+ tokens (* (- dnow ts) per-ms)))
    {:tokens (math/min (* 1.0 burst) refilled) :ts dnow}))

(defn spend (bucket cfg cost dnow)
  "Try to spend `cost` tokens from a refilled `bucket`. Returns `[new-bucket :ok]` when it had
  enough, else `[unchanged-bucket [:deny retry-ms]]` with the ms until `cost` tokens accrue."
  (let (b (refill bucket cfg dnow)
        tokens (get b :tokens))
    (if (>= tokens cost)
      [(assoc b :tokens (- tokens cost)) :ok]
      (let (per-ms (/ (get cfg :rate) (* 1.0 (get cfg :per)))
            deficit (- cost tokens)
            retry-ms (math/ceil (/ deficit per-ms)))
        [b [:deny retry-ms]]))))

(defn- ratelimit-loop (buckets)
  "Serialize every bucket read-refill-spend. `[:take key cost cfg dnow from r]` replies
  `[:take r :ok]` or `[:take r [:deny retry-ms]]`; `[:reset from r]` clears all buckets (tests)."
  (receive
    ([:take key cost cfg dnow from r] (let (result (spend (get buckets key {}) cfg cost dnow)
                                            bucket (nth result 0)
                                            decision (nth result 1))
                                        (send from [:take r decision])
                                        (ratelimit-loop (assoc buckets key bucket))))
    ([:reset from r] (do (send from [:reset r]) (ratelimit-loop {})))
    ;; How many keys are held. Worth being able to ask: the bucket table is keyed by
    ;; whatever `:key-fn` returns, so a key derived from user input grows without bound and
    ;; there is otherwise no way to see it happening.
    ([:count from r] (do (send from [:count r (count (keys buckets))]) (ratelimit-loop buckets)))))

(defn limiter ()
  "The rate-limit counter pid — a supervised singleton, started on first use. A crash resets the
  buckets (ephemeral state; the worst case is one window of over-admission), then it recovers."
  (ensure-supervised :hatch-ratelimit-sup
    [{:id :hatch-ratelimit
      :name :hatch-ratelimit
      :restart :permanent
      :start (fn () (spawn (ratelimit-loop {})))}]
    :hatch-ratelimit))

(defn take-token (key cost cfg &optional (now (os/now)))
  "Ask the in-memory limiter to spend `cost` tokens from `key`'s bucket. Returns `:ok` or
  `[:deny retry-ms]`. The store contract's `:take`, and usable directly.

  Fails OPEN (`:ok`) if the limiter does not answer within a second. A limiter hiccup must
  not take the endpoint down: the worst case of admitting a window of traffic is a busy
  server, and the worst case of the alternative is an outage caused by the thing meant to
  prevent one.

  `now` is taken as an argument rather than read inside the process, so the CALLER's clock
  drives refill — which is what lets `spend` be tested without a running limiter, and what
  lets an alternative store be exercised at a time of the test's choosing."
  (let (r (ref))
    (send (limiter) [:take key cost cfg now (self) r])
    (receive
      ([:take ^r decision] decision)
      (after 1000 :ok))))

; ---- counters ----
;;
;; A limiter that never denies and a limiter nobody reaches look identical from outside, and
;; both look like one that is working. Kept here rather than derived from telemetry so the
;; numbers exist whether or not an app attached a listener.

(def- *counters* (table/new))

(defn- bump (k)
  (table/put *counters* k (+ 1 (table/get *counters* k 0))))

(defn bucket-count ()
  "How many keys the in-memory store currently holds, or nil if it does not answer promptly.

  The number to watch. Buckets are keyed by whatever `:key-fn` returns and are never
  evicted, so a key taken from user input — a header, a path, an account id — grows the
  table without bound. A steadily climbing count is that bug, visible before it is a memory
  problem."
  (let (r (ref))
    (send (limiter) [:count (self) r])
    (receive
      ([:count ^r n] n)
      (after 1000 nil))))

(defn stats ()
  "Counters for a dashboard: `{:allowed :denied :deny-rate :buckets}`.

  `:deny-rate` is a percentage of decisions made, or nil when none have been — no traffic is
  not a healthy zero, and showing 0% for it invites the wrong conclusion. It is a NUMBER and
  not always a float: `round-to` demotes a whole value, so 25.0 comes back as 25. Format it,
  do not compare it to a float literal.

  `:buckets` is nil when the limiter does not answer promptly — it fails open, so a reader
  that cannot reach it should say so rather than print a confident zero."
  (let (allowed (table/get *counters* :allowed 0)
        denied (table/get *counters* :denied 0)
        total (+ allowed denied))
    {:allowed allowed
     :denied denied
     :deny-rate (if (= total 0) nil (math/round-to (* 100.0 (/ denied (* 1.0 total))) 1))
     :buckets (bucket-count)}))

(defn reset-stats ()
  "Zero the counters (tests, and a dashboard that wants a fresh window)."
  (table/put *counters* :allowed 0)
  (table/put *counters* :denied 0)
  nil)

; ---- the store seam ----

;; The default bucket store: one supervised process per node, holding every key's bucket.
;;
;; **Per NODE.** Each machine runs its own, so a client round-robined across N machines gets
;; N times the configured allowance — measured on a two-machine deployment as a burst of 20
;; admitting 40. That is a deliberate trade, not an oversight: a rate-limit decision sits on
;; the request path, and making it a network call spends a round trip on every limited
;; request to avoid over-admitting by a factor of the machine count. `web/cache` refuses the
;; same trade for the same reason.
;;
;; So: divide your intended ceiling by the machine count, or swap in a shared `:store` when
;; you need a ceiling you can prove. See the module docstring for the contract.
(def in-memory-store {:take take-token})

; ---- key selection ----

(defn client-key (conn)
  "The rate-limit key for `conn`: the client's address if a proxy named it, else the constant
  `\"global\"` (one shared bucket). Behind a proxy, set one of the client-IP headers or every
  client shares the global bucket.

  `web/auth/client-ip` decides which header that is — `Fly-Client-IP`, `X-Real-IP`,
  `CF-Connecting-IP`, then the first hop of `X-Forwarded-For`. This module used to answer the
  question itself and answered it differently: it did not look at `Fly-Client-IP` at all, and
  it preferred `X-Forwarded-For` — which the CLIENT can write — over the headers a proxy
  overwrites. Whichever header identifies a caller for the auth gate is the one that should
  identify them for the rate limiter."
  (or (web/auth/client-ip conn) "global"))

(def *ipv6-prefix-groups* 4)

(defn network-key (conn)
  "Like [[client-key]], but an IPv6 client is keyed by its **/64 network** rather than its
  exact address.

  An address is not a client. IPv6 privacy extensions rotate the host half of an address —
  the same machine on the same network appears as `2c0f:ef18:1b2e:0:231b:…` one day and
  `2c0f:ef18:1b2e:0:5d71:…` the next, and a determined caller can cycle through them far
  faster than that. Keyed per address, a bucket is trivially escaped by picking a new one;
  keyed per /64, the whole interface shares one. `web/auth/allow-ips` already treats a /64
  as the unit of identity for the same reason.

  The cost is bluntness in the other direction: everyone behind one /64 shares a bucket.
  That is the right trade for an abuse control on a public read endpoint, and the wrong one
  for anything where a neighbour's traffic must not affect yours — so [[client-key]] stays
  the default and this is opt-in:

      (ratelimit/rate-limit {:rate 300 :per 60000 :key-fn ratelimit/network-key})

  IPv4 is returned unchanged: there is no equivalent host/network split to take, and a v4
  address is already one customer far more often than not."
  (let (address (web/auth/client-ip conn))
    (cond
      (nil? address) "global"
      (not (includes? address ":")) address
      else (or (ipv6-network address) address))))

(defn ipv6-network (address)
  "The `/64` network of IPv6 `address` — its first four groups, expanded and zero-padded,
  as a key. nil when `address` is not IPv6 or cannot be read as eight groups.

      (hatch/web/ratelimit/ipv6-network \"2c0f:ef18:1b2e:0:231b:1:2:3\") ;=> \"2c0f:ef18:1b2e:0000::/64\"
      (hatch/web/ratelimit/ipv6-network \"::1\") ;=> \"0000:0000:0000:0000::/64\"
      (hatch/web/ratelimit/ipv6-network \"10.0.0.1\") ;=> nil"
  (let (groups (ipv6-groups (unbracket address)))
    (if (nil? groups)
      nil
      (str (string/join (into [] (take groups *ipv6-prefix-groups*)) ":") "::/64"))))

(defn- unbracket (address)
  "`address` without a `[…]` wrapper and without a trailing `:port` on a bracketed form."
  (let (lower (string/lower (string/trim address)))
    (if (string/starts-with? lower "[")
      (let (close (index-of lower "]")) (if (< close 0) lower (string/substring lower 1 close)))
      lower)))

(defn- ipv6-groups (address)
  "IPv6 `address` as its eight 16-bit groups, lowercased and zero-padded, expanding a `::`
  run. nil for anything that is not IPv6."
  (if (< (count (filter (string/->list address) (fn (c) (= c ":")))) 2)
    nil
    (let (halves (string/split address "::")
          head (filter (string/split (first halves) ":") (fn (g) (not (= g ""))))
          after (second halves)
          tail (if (nil? after) [] (filter (string/split after ":") (fn (g) (not (= g "")))))
          gap (- 8 (+ (count head) (count tail)))
          filled (append (into [] head) (map (range (math/max 0 gap)) (fn (_g) "0")) (into [] tail)))
      (if (= (count filled) 8)
        (apply vector (map filled (fn (g) (string/pad-left (string/lower g) 4 "0"))))
        nil))))

; ---- the plug ----

(defn limited (conn retry-after)
  "The default throttled response: a halted `429 Too Many Requests` with a `Retry-After`
  (whole seconds)."
  (halt (send-resp (put-resp-header (put-resp-header conn :retry-after (str retry-after))
                     :content-type "text/plain; charset=utf-8")
          429 "429 Too Many Requests\n")))

(defn bucket-config (opts)
  "The `{:rate :per :burst}` a store needs, from a plug's `opts`. Exposed so an adaptor can
  read the same configuration the built-in store does rather than reinvent the defaults."
  (let (rate (get opts :rate 60))
    {:rate rate :per (get opts :per 60000) :burst (get opts :burst rate)}))

(defn rate-limit (&optional (opts {}))
  "Plug factory: returns a plug that token-bucket rate-limits a request. Under the limit the
  conn passes through; over it, the conn is halted with a 429 and a `Retry-After`.

  Every part of the decision is replaceable — see the module docstring for the four seams
  and their contracts. Emits `[:hatch :ratelimit :denied]` telemetry on a rejection, so a
  429 storm is visible to `web/metrics` and a dashboard rather than only to the access log."
  (let (cfg (bucket-config opts)
        cost (get opts :cost 1)
        key-fn (get opts :key-fn client-key)
        on-limit (get opts :on-limit limited)
        skip? (get opts :skip? nil)
        store (get opts :store in-memory-store))
    (fn (conn)
      (if (and skip? (skip? conn))
        conn
        (let (key (key-fn conn)
              ;; A number or a function of the conn — an endpoint whose work scales with a
              ;; parameter can charge for it (a page size, a batch length) instead of
              ;; pretending every request costs the same.
              spent (if (fn? cost) (cost conn) cost))
          (match ((get store :take) key spent cfg (os/now))
            (:ok (do (bump :allowed) conn))
            ([:deny retry-ms] (let (retry-after (math/max 1 (math/ceil (/ retry-ms 1000.0))))
                                (bump :denied)
                                (telemetry/emit [:hatch :ratelimit :denied]
                                  {:retry_after retry-after}
                                  {:key key
                                   :cost spent
                                   :method (get conn :method)
                                   :path (get conn :path)})
                                (on-limit conn retry-after)))))))))
